2. the bad set's width in D, at epsp = 1/2 (worst), as a function of sigma*h^2
      sigma*h^2    D_bad_max                         note
           0.01     0.495075               first A = pi/4
            0.1      0.45633               first A = pi/4
              1     0.277985               first A = pi/4
             10    0.0655125               first A = pi/4
            100       0.0077               first A = pi/4
           1000     0.000785               first A = pi/4
          10000        8e-05               first A = pi/4
          1e+06      2.5e-06               first A = pi/4
   the bad neighbourhood of D = 0 shrinks like 1/(sigma h^2): a window with a
   larger sigma pushes it down, and sigma ~ 1/(pi^2 (m+1)) with m+1 = lam*h,
   so sigma*h^2 = h/(lam pi^2) and the bad width is ~ lam pi^2/(4 epsp h).

3. what the bad width has to beat: the height gap to the nearest other zero.
   average gap at height T is 2 pi / log T. Setting lam pi^2/(4 epsp h) < 2 pi / log T
   T=1000     lam=1     eps'=0.5   gap=0.9096  h > 5.425
   T=1000     lam=1     eps'=0.1   gap=0.9096  h > 27.13
   T=1000     lam=100   eps'=0.5   gap=0.9096  h > 542.5
   T=1000     lam=100   eps'=0.1   gap=0.9096  h > 2713
   T=1e+06    lam=1     eps'=0.5   gap=0.4548  h > 10.85
   T=1e+06    lam=1     eps'=0.1   gap=0.4548  h > 54.25
   T=1e+06    lam=100   eps'=0.5   gap=0.4548  h > 1085
   T=1e+06    lam=100   eps'=0.1   gap=0.4548  h > 5425
   T=1e+12    lam=1     eps'=0.5   gap=0.2274  h > 21.7
   T=1e+12    lam=1     eps'=0.1   gap=0.2274  h > 108.5
   T=1e+12    lam=100   eps'=0.5   gap=0.2274  h > 2170
   T=1e+12    lam=100   eps'=0.1   gap=0.2274  h > 1.085e+04
   h grows only linearly in lam and in log T: this is a threshold, not a barrier.

4. CORRECTION to 2: the phase keeps turning, so the bad set is not a
   neighbourhood of D = 0 but a union of stripes. Measure of the bad set
   (cos(2A) > 0) over D in [0, 1/2], at eps' = 1/2:
      sigma*h^2   bad measure  as fraction   stripe width
           0.01      0.495073       0.9901            157
              1      0.277984       0.5560           1.57
            100      0.252574       0.5051         0.0157
          10000      0.249962       0.4999       0.000157
          1e+06      0.250001       0.5000       1.57e-06
   the fraction tends to 1/2: half the box hurts, in stripes that get finer
   as sigma*h^2 grows. Shrinking the stripes does not shrink the measure.

5. so the window question answers NO in the strong form and NO in the
   weak form: no window of this shape makes the off-line sign fixed, and
   no choice of h confines the hurting zeros to a small set. The phase
   turning IS the obstruction, and sigma only sets how fast it turns.

6. WHY. The boost and the phase are the same object. For any window of
   this shape the transform carries exp(w^2 * sigma), and
      |exp(w^2 sigma)| = exp((eps'^2 - D^2) h^2 sigma)   <- the boost
      arg exp(w^2 sigma) = 2 eps' D h^2 sigma             <- the phase
   one sigma, both effects. Detection needs the boost at the target,
   which forces the sweep at every other off-line zero.
      sigma*h^2   log boost at D=0   phase per unit D
              1               0.25                  1
            100                 25                100
          10000               2500              1e+04
          1e+06            2.5e+05              1e+06
   the boost is exp(eps'^2 X) and the sweep is 2 eps' X: making the boost
   big makes the sweep fast, at the same rate in X. sigma = 0 removes the
   sweep (sign becomes (eps'^2 - D^2)/(eps'^2 + D^2), fixed) and removes
   the boost with it, so the target no longer beats the background.
